1. Why registers are important ?

	Registers are high speed storagr inside the CPU 

	In ARM architecture 

		* Most operations are performed on registers
		* Memory access is minimized 
		* Faster execution is achieved 

	ARM follows a load store architecture, so understanding registers is critical 

2. Overview of ARM7 Registers 

	ARM7 provides 16 programmer visible registers, each 32 bit wide 

	They are named : 

		R0 to R15 

	Each register has a specific role, even though most are general purpose

3. Classification of ARM Registers

	ARM registers can be classified into : 

		1. General Purpose Registers
		2. Special Purpose Registers 
		3. Banked Registers

4. General Purpose Registers (R0 - R12)

	R0 - R12 

		* Used for data manipulation 
		* Used to store operands and results 
		* Can be freely used by the programer 

	Typical Usage : 

		* R0 - R3  : Function arguments and return values 
		* R4 - R11 : Local variables 
		* R12 	   : Intra-procedure scratch register 

5. Special Purpose Registers 

	R13 - Stack Pointer (SP)
		
		* Points to the top of the stack 
		* Used during function calls, interrupts, and exceptions 
		* Stack usually grows downwards in memory 

	Each processor mode has its own SP.

	R14 - Link Register (LR)

		* Stores the return address during function call 
		* Automatically updated during BL (Branch with Link) instruction

	On exception entry, LR stores the return address of the interrupted code.

	R15 - Program Counter (PC)
	
		* Holds the address of the current instruction
		* Automatically updated after each instruction

	Important Pipeline behaviour :

		Due to pipelining : 

			* In ARM state, PC = Current instruction address + 8
			* In THUMB state, PC = Current instruction address +4

		This behaviour is very important for understanding branches and debugging 

6. Banked Registers Concept 

	ARM Supports banked registers to support fast exception handling

	What are banked registers ?

		* Certain registers have seperate physical copies for different modes 
		* Avoids saving/restoring registers during interrupts 
		* Improves response time 

7. Processor Modes and Banked registers

	* ARM7 has multiple processor modes : 

		* User 
		* FIQ 
		* IRQ
		* Supervisor (SVC)
		* Abort
		* Undefined 
		* System 

		Some modes have banked versions of 

			* R13 (SP)
			* R14 (LR)

		FIQ mode also has banked R8-R12

8. Register Banking in FIQ Mode 

	FIQ mode is designed for fast interrupt handling

	Banked registers in FIQ mode : 

		* R8_fiq
		* R9_fiq
		* R10_fiq
		* R11_fiq
		* R12_fiq
		* R13_fiq(SP)
		* R14_fiq(LR)

	This allows FIQ handler to run without saving registers 

9. Visible vs Hidden Registers 

	* Visible register : R0 - R15 (accessible to programmer)
	* Hidden register  : Banked refgisters (mode - specific)

	The processor automatically selects the correct register register bank based on the current mode. 

10. Register Summary Table 

	Register 	Name		Purpose 

	R0 - R12	GPRs		Data storage and operations

	R13		SP		Stack Pointer

	R14 		LR 		Link Register 

	R15 		PC 		Program Counter 

11. Why ARM Register Organization is Powerful ?

	* Large register set reduces memory access 
	* Banked registers enable fast interrupt handling 
	* Dedicated SP and LR simplify function calls 

	This makes ARM efficient for real time embedded systems 

12. Summary 

	* ARM7 has 16 programmer visible 32 bit registers
	* R0-R12 are general purpose
	* R13 (SP), R14(LR), and R15(PC) are special purpose 
	* Banked registers improve interrupt perfomance 
	* PC behaviour is affected by pipelining 





































		



































































 	
